    <string name="humidity_help">Relative humidity describes how much moisture the air holds relative to its capacity at this temperature. A higher dew point often feels more humid.</string>
    <string name="uv_help">UV measures the strength of ultraviolet radiation. Higher values call for more sun protection. This is the forecast, not a personal exposure measurement.</string>
    <string name="pressure_help">Sea-level pressure removes the effect of elevation, making weather systems easier to compare. Surface pressure is the pressure at local ground level.</string>
    <string name="wind_help">Wind direction indicates where the wind comes from. Gusts are short peaks and can be stronger than the sustained wind.</string>
    <string name="aqi_help">US AQI and European AQI use different scales. Pollutant concentrations are shown in μg/m³. Regional model estimates can differ from nearby monitors.</string>
    <string name="precip_help">Precipitation is the accumulation for each interval, not a probability. Fifteen-minute values can be interpolated from hourly forecasts outside high-resolution coverage.</string>
    <string name="visibility_help">Visibility estimates how far objects can be distinguished. Fog, precipitation, and particles can reduce it.</string>
